From: GloriousEggroll <gloriouseggroll@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: Preserve XEmbed tray interactions on Mutter.

The SNI backend intercepts Shell_NotifyIcon for X11 applications as well as
Wayland applications. GNOME's AppIndicator extension expects a D-Bus menu
instead of calling ContextMenu, but we advertise /NO_DBUSMENU and render
the application's own Win32 menu. Right-click therefore does nothing.

The extension also sends Activate once per physical double-click. Our raw
click emulation counts two Activate calls as a double-click, so applications
such as Battle.net require four clicks to reopen their window.

Let winex11 applications under Mutter retain explorer's existing XEmbed
backend, which forwards the actual mouse events. The driver query matches
GNOME Shell through winex11's existing Mutter name normalization. It does
not match winewayland, and does not change SNI on other window managers or
the independent desktop notification path.

Fixes: https://github.com/GloriousEggroll/proton-ge-custom/issues/803
References: https://github.com/ubuntu/gnome-shell-extension-appindicator/blob/master/indicatorStatusIcon.js
References: https://github.com/ubuntu/gnome-shell-extension-appindicator/blob/master/appIndicator.js
---
 dlls/win32u/sni.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/dlls/win32u/sni.c b/dlls/win32u/sni.c
--- a/dlls/win32u/sni.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/sni.c
@@ -1220,6 +1220,15 @@ LRESULT sni_notify_icon( HWND owner, UINT msg, NOTIFYICONDATAW *nid )
     struct sni_icon *icon, *target = NULL;
     LRESULT ret = -1;
 
+    /* GNOME's AppIndicator host requires a D-Bus menu and already translates
+     * double-clicks into Activate. Use explorer's XEmbed path on Mutter/X11;
+     * our native-menu SNI backend cannot preserve those interactions there. */
+    if (user_driver->pHasWindowManager( "Mutter" ))
+    {
+        if (msg == NIM_ADD) TRACE( "using XEmbed tray instead of SNI on Mutter\n" );
+        return -1;
+    }
+
     pthread_mutex_lock( &sni_mutex );
     if (!sni_init_done) sni_init();
     /* the watcher may have started after us. Re-probe on a fresh NIM_ADD rather
